{"name":"Aluminium Foil","hex":"#d2d9db","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aluminium foil","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very thin, pliable, easily torn sheet of aluminium used for cooking, packaging, cosmetics, and insulation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aluminium_foil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aluminium foil","blocks":[{"text":"Aluminium foil is aluminium prepared in thin metal leaves. The foil is pliable and can be readily bent or wrapped around objects. Thin foils are fragile and are sometimes laminated with other materials such as plastics or paper to make them stronger and more useful."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Aluminio.jpg/330px-Aluminio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium_foil","wikidata":"Q447349"},{"title":"Foil (metal)","blocks":[{"text":"A foil is a very thin sheet of metal, typically made by hammering or rolling. Foils are most easily made with malleable metal, such as aluminium, copper, tin, and gold. Foils usually bend under their own weight and can be torn easily. For example, aluminium foil is usually about 1⁄1000 inch (0.025 mm), whereas gold can be made into foil only a few atoms thick, called gold leaf. Extremely thin foil is called metal leaf. Leaf tears very easily and must be picked up with special brushes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Aluminio.jpg/330px-Aluminio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foil_(metal)","wikidata":"Q251473"},{"title":"Aluminium","blocks":[{"text":"Aluminium or aluminum is a chemical element; it has symbol Al and atomic number 13. It has a density lower than other common metals, about one-third that of steel. Aluminium has a great affinity toward oxygen, forming a protective layer of oxide on the surface when exposed to air. It visually resembles silver, both in its color and in its great ability to reflect light. It is soft, nonmagnetic, and ductile. It has one stable isotope, 27Al, which is highly abundant, making aluminium the 12th-most abundant element in the universe. The radioactivity of 26Al leads to it being used in radiometric dating."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Aluminium-4.jpg/330px-Aluminium-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium","wikidata":"Q663"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Rubber_with_sodium_silicate_beads_6_alu_backside.jpg/500px-Rubber_with_sodium_silicate_beads_6_alu_backside.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Rubber_with_sodium_silicate_beads_6_alu_backside.jpg/1280px-Rubber_with_sodium_silicate_beads_6_alu_backside.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Rubber_with_sodium_silicate_beads_6_alu_backside.jpg/500px-Rubber_with_sodium_silicate_beads_6_alu_backside.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Microscopic close-up of aluminium foil on the back of an intumescent rubber strip","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARubber_with_sodium_silicate_beads_6_alu_backside.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Aluminium_foil_micrometer.jpg/500px-Aluminium_foil_micrometer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Aluminium_foil_micrometer.jpg/500px-Aluminium_foil_micrometer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A roll of aluminium foil, with micrometer showing a thickness of 13